# /usr/share/pam-configs/nostr — pam-auth-update profile for pam_nostr.so
#
# Registers the broker-backed nostr PAM module with Debian's pam-auth-update
# framework so admins can enable it declaratively with
#
#   sudo pam-auth-update
#
# instead of overwriting gdm-password or common-auth by hand. pam-auth-update
# composes the Auth-Type/Account-Type blocks below into the standard PAM
# includes (common-auth, common-account) that gdm-password, sshd, sudo etc.
# already `@include`, so no gdm3-owned conffile is ever touched.
#
# Semantics mirror the shipped packaging/pam/gdm-password.sample exactly —
# the deliberate strict-deny posture from beads nostrc-o1ho:
#
#   success        -> done   : broker accepted the proof; stack proceeds and
#                              the Unix stack (`pam_unix`) is NOT consulted.
#   user_unknown   -> ignore : account is not a nostr account (broker returned
#                              NH_AUTH_RESULT_UNKNOWN_ACCOUNT); fall through
#                              to common-auth so ordinary local users log in.
#   authinfo_unavail -> ignore : broker unreachable / transport error; fall
#                              through so a stopped broker never bricks local
#                              login. A nostr-only account will then simply
#                              fail at common-auth.
#   default        -> die    : a KNOWN nostr account that FAILED its proof
#                              (invalid_proof / denied / rate-limited /
#                              disabled -> PAM_AUTH_ERR / PAM_MAXTRIES /
#                              PAM_ACCT_EXPIRED) denies immediately. It MUST
#                              NOT fall through to Unix password auth,
#                              otherwise a wrong nostr proof could be bypassed
#                              by a local password.
#
# `Default: no` keeps activation an explicit admin act (rollback = re-run
# pam-auth-update and untick nostr, or `pam-auth-update --package --remove nostr`
# on package removal). Priority 384 places pam_nostr ABOVE pam_unix (priority
# 256) so the broker gets first look for nostr accounts before Unix ever runs.
#
# Prerequisites (see docs/INSTALLED_LOGIN.md):
#   - nostr-authd running with auth.sock at /run/nostr-auth/auth.sock
#   - libnss_nostr.so.2 on glibc's search path
#   - /etc/nsswitch.conf lists `nostr` on passwd and group lines
#   - /etc/nss_nostr.conf points at the read-only projection nss.db
Name: Nostr authentication (broker-backed)
Default: no
Priority: 384
Auth-Type: Primary
Auth:
	[success=end new_authtok_reqd=ok user_unknown=ignore authinfo_unavail=ignore default=die]	pam_nostr.so socket=/run/nostr-auth/auth.sock
Auth-Initial:
	[success=end new_authtok_reqd=ok user_unknown=ignore authinfo_unavail=ignore default=die]	pam_nostr.so socket=/run/nostr-auth/auth.sock
Account-Type: Primary
Account:
	[success=end user_unknown=ignore authinfo_unavail=ignore default=die]	pam_nostr.so socket=/run/nostr-auth/auth.sock
Account-Initial:
	[success=end user_unknown=ignore authinfo_unavail=ignore default=die]	pam_nostr.so socket=/run/nostr-auth/auth.sock
